WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.159 --> 00:00:04.051
Han Solo: Let's blow
this thing and go home!

00:00:04.051 --> 00:00:05.570
Garrett Reisman: "Pwsh!"

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.330
Hi, I'm Garrett Reisman,
former NASA astronaut.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.313
And today we're gonna look
at some popular movie clips,

00:00:12.313 --> 00:00:13.920
and I'll try to use my experience

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.850
as a former director of space operations

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.340
at SpaceX and current professor

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.830
at University of Southern California, USC,

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:23.380
and give you a rating
on how realistic it is.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.213
Ryan Stone: No, no, no, no!

00:00:26.962 --> 00:00:28.199
Gotcha.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.947
Matt Kowalski: You have to
let me go, or we both die.

00:00:34.947 --> 00:00:37.355
Stone: I'm not letting you go. We're fine!

00:00:37.355 --> 00:00:38.605
Kowalski: No.

00:00:40.204 --> 00:00:41.340
Stone: No!

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.507
Garrett: Oh, my goodness.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.303
Oh.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.590
Yeah, that was bogus. [laughs]

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.250
I really like this movie, despite the fact

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.610
that the science, totally wrong.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:57.150
This movie takes great liberties

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.040
and completely blows off
the basic laws of physics.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.789
Kowalski: Man down!
Stone: Copy.

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:02.622
Houston.

00:01:02.622 --> 00:01:04.600
Garrett: The movie
deals with space debris,

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.050
and that's what causes the calamity

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.430
that they then have to
fight the rest of the movie.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.890
This is a real problem,
and it's getting worse.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.100
So, some of the stuff is natural,

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.000
but the vast bulk of the flux,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.290
the density of the stuff
whizzing around the Earth,

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:19.240
is created by humans.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.880
It's stuff that we shot up into space,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.660
and we didn't clean up our own mess.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.553
When I was up there, several times

00:01:24.553 --> 00:01:27.620
I heard debris hit the space station.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:28.720
There's certain things in the movie

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.160
about how they show the
debris that's not accurate.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.550
You can see it coming in the movie.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.300
They see this, like,
cloud of debris coming

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.560
from thousands of kilometers away.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.130
That's bogus, OK?

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.980
This stuff is traveling
an order of magnitude

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.850
like 10 times faster than a rifle bullet.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.960
You can't look at a shooting range and see

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.940
a rifle bullet flying
around from thousands

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:47.950
of kilometers away.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.370
You're not gonna see this stuff coming.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:50.672
And the other thing about this that's

00:01:50.672 --> 00:01:52.190
completely unrealistic: George Clooney

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.490
is flying on by, and
Sandra Bullock reaches

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.100
and grabs his tether and grabs onto him.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:58.970
That would be hard.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.000
As he's flying on by, he
would, with his momentum,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.930
try to carry her away and pull her away

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.287
from the space station,
and she would have to

00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:09.100
resist that and stop him, which she does.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.050
Yay, Sandra Bullock.
You have saved the day.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.490
Well done.

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:14.400
End of story.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.410
It should end right there.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.730
There's no gravity, right,
that's pulling him away.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.770
It's not like she's holding him, like,

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.250
over a cliff or something.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:23.940
He is not pulling her off
the space station anymore.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:24.860
The work is done.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.680
And once she has stopped his motion

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.870
and he's just sitting there,

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:31.050
and she's holding on to the
other end of the tether,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.190
all she has to do is go like this

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.760
with her littlest pinky.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.170
"Boop!" And pull him,
and he would be going

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.590
right back to the space station,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.520
and they'd be having
a nice lunch together.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.810
He says, "You have to let me go."

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:42.110
Why?

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.450
Why does she have to let him go?

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:47.350
And you've got a firm
grip on George Clooney.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.390
You do not let go.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.756
OK? No matter what the
laws of physics say,

00:02:51.756 --> 00:02:52.871
you hold on.

00:02:57.571 --> 00:02:59.522
Mark Watney: On my way, commander.

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.540
Garrett: This whole idea
about puncturing your glove

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.060
to fly like Iron Man and be rescued

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.700
by your spaceship.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:10.720
Yeah, not so much.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.240
We have a jet pack that we wear

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.010
when we do spacewalks
on the space station.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.840
We needed to have something to do

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.850
if you became untethered and started

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.380
floating off and you're gonna become lost

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.800
in space during a spacewalk.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.210
That's why we added these jet packs.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.605
It's just nitrogen. And
it's just little puffs,

00:03:24.605 --> 00:03:26.300
"psh, psh, psh," of nitrogen that come out

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.450
of those little jets

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.474
is enough to let you fly
back to the space station

00:03:30.474 --> 00:03:32.310
and stop you from being lost in space.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.440
Now, first of all, you
would need a spacesuit

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.170
pressurized really, really high to get

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:37.720
enough pressure to actually get the kind

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:38.830
of thrust that he gets.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:42.160
In reality, it's gonna be
a tiny little gentle push.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.630
There's not gonna be a "shh!"

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:45.990
It's gonna be a "pew, pew, pew, pew."

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:47.073
You know, come on.

00:03:47.073 --> 00:03:48.560
So that's the first thing that's wrong.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.240
But if you do have this big rocket engine

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.240
coming outta your hand, and you're trying

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.560
to control yourself, now you've gotta put

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.810
that thrust through
your center of gravity.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:58.490
So you gotta hold it very, very precisely,

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:00.985
and any kind of motion

00:04:00.985 --> 00:04:03.138
away from the proper direction

00:04:03.138 --> 00:04:05.040
is gonna cause you to start
tumbling out of control.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.600
You're gonna be like, if you
take a balloon and blow it up

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.172
and then just release it and it goes

00:04:09.172 --> 00:04:11.247
"pbbt" all over the place,

00:04:11.247 --> 00:04:13.271
no way you're gonna be, like, "zhhh,"

00:04:13.271 --> 00:04:15.162
flying back where you wanna go.

00:04:15.162 --> 00:04:18.263
So, bogus, big-time bogus on that.

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:21.930
The rest of the movie
was up, like, a nine.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.445
The rest of the movie
was really, really good.

00:04:23.445 --> 00:04:24.480
Solo: You're all clear, kid.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.356
Now let's blow this thing and go home.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:40.710
Garrett: The scene in "Star
Wars" where Luke Skywalker

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.370
uses the force and destroys the Death Star

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.450
by attacking its one hidden weakness.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.330
So, a couple things pop out right away.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:50.257
One is, you know, what's
with the orange ski goggles

00:04:50.257 --> 00:04:52.210
on Luke in the middle of black space?

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.940
I mean, really, you need that?

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.550
And then, finally, like, Han
Solo's wearing this earpiece

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:57.540
with that microphone thing.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:58.800
It looks like he's a telemarketer.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.170
He looks like he's trying to sell you

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.390
some insurance or something.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:05.310
But the big thing is they
blow up the Death Star, right,

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.275
which is awesome, and it goes "pwsh!"

00:05:08.275 --> 00:05:09.710
Now, that wouldn't happen.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.530
I mean, you're not gonna hear anything

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.020
because sound needs a
medium to propagate through.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:16.547
So right now, you're
hearing me talk because

00:05:16.547 --> 00:05:19.547
as vibrations go on in my throat

00:05:19.547 --> 00:05:22.238
and air goes through that and
the air molecules vibrate,

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:23.981
they vibrate other air molecules,

00:05:23.981 --> 00:05:26.507
and because we have air in this room,

00:05:26.507 --> 00:05:27.949
it reaches the microphone

00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:29.770
and causes vibrations on the microphone.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.253
And then you hear that.

00:05:31.253 --> 00:05:32.470
If there's no air here,

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.200
But other than that, you
wouldn't be able to hear me.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:40.770
The thing is that if
you're watching a movie

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:43.436
and you see a big
explosion and it's silent,

00:05:43.436 --> 00:05:44.900
it doesn't feel right to the audience.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.999
So I understand why movie editors

00:05:47.999 --> 00:05:49.810
and special-effects guys put the sound in,

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:51.220
but it's bogus.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:54.180
You can see explosions,
and there can be fire,

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.280
even in the vacuum of space.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:57.370
You actually need three things.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:58.370
You need spark.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.700
You need fuel.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.995
And then you need oxygen
for it to combust.

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:04.730
Then you will see flame,
you will see fire,

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.130
even in the vacuum of space.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:07.530
You won't hear anything,

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.540
but you'll see it.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:09.816
So that is plausible.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:17.580
Garrett: That McConaughey guy?

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:18.890
He looks just like me, huh?

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:20.550
So, I'd say, obviously, the casting people

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.022
know what they're doing.

00:06:22.022 --> 00:06:23.179
Cooper: TARS?

00:06:23.179 --> 00:06:24.566
TARS: Roger that.

00:06:25.782 --> 00:06:27.483
Cooper: Don't you get it yet, TARS?

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:32.890
That's why I'm here.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:34.480
I'm gonna find a way to tell Murph.

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:36.095
TARS: How, Cooper?

00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:37.343
Cooper: The watch.

00:06:38.375 --> 00:06:39.469
The watch.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:42.650
Garrett: Wow, so that
was pretty trippy, huh?

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:43.770
If I remember right from the movie,

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.450
he's inside a black hole at this point

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:48.000
where the forces of gravity are so strong

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.620
that not even light can escape.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:51.960
Recently, we got our first
picture of a black hole.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.870
Really, what's around the black hole,

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:56.440
because once light goes
into the event horizon,

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:58.020
the circle around the black hole,

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:58.910
nothing comes out.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:00.950
But inside the black hole, nobody knows

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:02.310
what happens inside there.

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:03.550
Nothing ever comes back.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.210
If a person went inside a black hole,

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:06.200
what would it really be like?

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:07.110
Who knows?

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.330
But you do see the light bending in there,

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:11.130
and it is true that light will bend

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:14.040
when it's exposed to very
strong gravitational fields.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.820
A lot of the stuff that
they did in this movie,

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:17.700
they tried to be as realistic as possible,

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:20.292
but I do have a problem with...

00:07:20.292 --> 00:07:22.880
there's this bookshelf tesseract thing.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.880
So, some advanced civilization created

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.032
this tesseract where
you go into this thing

00:07:27.032 --> 00:07:28.790
and it's, like, lined
with books on the shelf,

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:31.027
and by pulling the books off the shelf,

00:07:31.027 --> 00:07:33.466
you could see into other dimensions

00:07:33.466 --> 00:07:35.970
and you could communicate to people

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.160
in those other dimensions in a very, very

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:41.460
roundabout, incredibly complicated way.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.180
If you're a super-advanced
alien intelligence

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:47.850
that can build a bookshelf tesseract thing

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:50.270
to go across dimensions,

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.590
why can't you put in a phone?

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:53.698
Or at least put in, like,
a whiteboard, right?

00:07:53.698 --> 00:07:56.465
With a dry-erase marker that
you could just spell, like,

00:07:56.465 --> 00:07:59.140
"Hey! Stay away from the black hole!"

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:01.580
You know, whatever, and
send a message that way.

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:03.040
Why do you have to get so complicated?

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:05.220
But other than that, they
got a lot of stuff right,

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:07.487
especially about the relativistic
effects in the movie.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:23.397
Garrett: This is one of my
all-time favorite space films.

00:08:23.397 --> 00:08:25.467
And what's really amazing about it is

00:08:25.467 --> 00:08:27.680
how realistic it is
considering they filmed

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:30.060
all of this before Apollo 11,

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:32.060
before we landed men on the moon.

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:35.300
And the science in the movie
holds up tremendously well.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:36.630
The one thing that's missing there,

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:39.220
and in the opening scene
there, is the atmosphere.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:40.620
You just see the blue Earth

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.670
and then suddenly the black space.

00:08:42.670 --> 00:08:44.525
In reality, when you look
at the Earth from space,

00:08:44.525 --> 00:08:46.348
there's a thin blue line that separates

00:08:46.348 --> 00:08:48.470
the sunlit Earth from
the blackness of space.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:49.440
So, you might be asking yourself,

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.010
why is the space station spinning

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:53.580
and why is it constructed as a big wheel?

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:55.416
What you're doing is you're creating

00:08:55.416 --> 00:08:58.020
inertial forces that simulate gravity

00:08:58.020 --> 00:08:59.480
and have the same effect.

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:00.730
Because the absence of gravity

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.750
causes some problems to the human body.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.100
It causes, for example, some bone loss.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:07.420
Now, you're up in space,
and you're floating

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:10.363
around like a fish,
and the bones are like,

00:09:10.363 --> 00:09:12.370
I guess I don't need
to make any more bone.

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:15.252
Your muscles, especially in
your legs, start to atrophy

00:09:15.252 --> 00:09:16.743
'cause you're not using your legs anymore.

00:09:16.743 --> 00:09:17.576
You're not walking.

00:09:17.576 --> 00:09:21.000
So all kinds of, like,
funky and not-so-good things

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.100
happen to your body in space,

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:24.100
so we fight it.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:26.530
We fight it hard, and
mostly by working out.

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:29.860
We have to do it about
two hours a day every day.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:32.490
But, hey, what if we can
create artificial gravity?

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:33.750
Then we wouldn't have to do any of this,

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:35.880
and we wouldn't have
any of these problems.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.920
That's the idea behind
the spinning spaceship.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.369
The best way I can explain that is

00:09:39.369 --> 00:09:40.705
they had these old amusement park rides

00:09:40.705 --> 00:09:42.088
that were like a big washing machine.

00:09:42.088 --> 00:09:44.240
You get in, and it starts spinning,

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:46.050
and it throws you up against the wall.

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:47.720
And then the floor drops out,

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:49.150
and you don't fall because you're being

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:52.100
pushed back against a
wall by a pseudo force

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.460
we call centrifugal force.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:55.710
That's what's happening
here in this space station.

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:59.605
By spinning like that, you
can create centrifugal force

00:09:59.605 --> 00:10:02.632
that acts and feels
like artificial gravity.

00:10:02.632 --> 00:10:05.401
If you're creating artificial
gravity with a small radius,

00:10:05.401 --> 00:10:07.470
like those rides in
amusement park that make you

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:09.130
go up against the wall, you can handle

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:11.070
that spinning for a couple minutes.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:13.210
You don't want to be spending
months in there, right?

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:15.443
You need to avoid having
the radius too small

00:10:15.443 --> 00:10:17.730
that you get what's
called Coriolis effects

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:19.130
that makes you kind of dizzy.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:19.963
You don't want that.

00:10:19.963 --> 00:10:21.520
You also need to be spinning fast enough

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.480
so that if you walk
the opposite direction,

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:24.930
you don't start floating.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:26.130
So you have to have a rim speed

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.880
that's typically about
6 meters per second,

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:30.502
which is bigger than your
average person walks.

00:10:30.502 --> 00:10:32.836
So, a lot of times in the movies,

00:10:32.836 --> 00:10:34.060
either it's not long enough

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.860
or it's spinning too slow
for it to really do its job.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:38.700
Does "2001" get it right?

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:39.620
Yes, it does.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:40.970
If you look at that thing

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:43.843
and you time the rate of the rotation

00:10:43.843 --> 00:10:45.840
and you try to estimate how big it is,

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:48.340
the radius is about 150 meters,

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:52.130
and it's spinning at about 1 1/2 rpm.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:54.885
That's enough to get you almost half a G.

00:10:54.885 --> 00:10:57.010
So almost half the gravity
that you feel on Earth,

00:10:57.010 --> 00:10:58.670
which is probably good enough.

00:10:58.670 --> 00:10:59.740
So that's not bad.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:01.310
If those numbers are accurate.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.620
Which, I kinda made it up, so, you know.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:04.995
But I think we're close.

00:11:18.648 --> 00:11:21.252
Rocket: Quill, don't be ridiculous.

00:11:21.252 --> 00:11:22.640
Get back into your pod!

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.220
You'll die in seconds!

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:26.930
Garrett: Hey, look, any
movie with a talking raccoon

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:29.163
is OK in my book.

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:32.461
Oh, boy.

00:11:32.461 --> 00:11:34.470
[laughs] OK.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:35.930
First of all, it's great that he's got

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:37.820
this mask on so he can breathe in space,

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:39.380
but what about the rest of his body?

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:41.060
The rest of your body is gonna be

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:43.650
in really, really bad shape.

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:46.810
The fluids inside your
tissues is gonna vaporize,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.960
and so basically, your
blood is gonna boil,

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.760
all the water in your tissue
is gonna turn into gas.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.500
He takes off his helmet to try to save

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:57.000
his friend there,
somehow holds his breath.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.730
Now, it's true that you can survive

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:01.830
for very brief periods of time

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:03.110
while exposed to a vacuum.

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:04.140
It has been done.

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:07.300
There's been accidents on
Earth in vacuum chambers,

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:10.210
and if you get quickly back down

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:13.050
to regular pressure, you're gonna be OK.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:14.366
But there's a couple
things you worry about.

00:12:14.366 --> 00:12:17.030
The first thing you have
to worry about: barotrauma.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:18.570
So, what that means is that there's gas

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:20.410
inside your body, in your lungs,

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:22.110
in your sinuses, and that's gonna start

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.240
expanding once you get
exposed to a vacuum,

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.340
'cause now, instead of having the outside

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:27.580
air pressure to work against,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:28.850
now there's nothing out here.

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:30.270
It's gonna want to blow up like a balloon.

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:32.360
Then you worry about
decompression sickness,

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:34.120
where all the nitrogen comes out

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:35.610
of the solution in your blood,

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:37.610
what scuba divers can get when they

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:40.750
spend too much time at depth
and come up too quickly.

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:41.650
They call it "the bends" 'cause you

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:43.380
bend over in pain at all your joints

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:44.600
where the bubbles build up.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:46.640
So all that would happen to Star-Lord

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:48.140
even before he takes off his mask.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:49.670
Having said that, did I mention

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:51.940
that this movie has a talking raccoon?

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:53.550
You're worried about the helmet?

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:55.392
I mean, it's got a talking raccoon.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:15.723
Garrett: True story, I use this
clip in my class here at USC

00:13:15.723 --> 00:13:17.742
when I was teaching this
subject about what happens

00:13:17.742 --> 00:13:20.118
if you do get exposed to
vacuum, and I tell people

00:13:20.118 --> 00:13:22.348
we actually have a test
subject that volunteered,

00:13:22.348 --> 00:13:24.210
and at NASA we did an experiment,

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:27.093
and we have video of that
experiment, and here it is.

00:13:27.093 --> 00:13:29.430
And I show that clip, and they
get a good laugh out of it.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:31.970
'Cause, no, that's not
really terribly accurate.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:33.460
What would happen if you're exposed

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:36.210
to space and were not
adequately protected,

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:37.820
and that is when parts of your body,

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:40.190
parts of your tissues,
the liquid vaporizes,

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:42.270
turns into a gas and you
puff up like a balloon.

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:44.900
Presumably, that would happen
around your face as well,

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:46.440
but it wouldn't look exactly like that.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:49.113
That's a little Hollywood
magic right there.

00:13:49.113 --> 00:13:50.120
Another thing about this clip

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:52.650
is that he hits a rock
and the visor shatters,

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:54.280
and you hear it shatter like it's glass.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:56.620
Most spacesuit helmets are
made out of polycarbonate,

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:57.880
which is much tougher.

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.420
It wouldn't shatter quite like that.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:01.510
You can break your space helmet,

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:04.430
and so tumbling down a surface on Mars

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:05.670
and whacking your head into a rock

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:07.004
is not recommended.

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:11.600
Barf: No, we're losing power!

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:13.500
Starr: Why?
Barf: 'Cause we're outta gas!

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:15.430
Starr: OK, we'll have to set her down.

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:17.355
Prepare for an emergency landing.

00:14:26.777 --> 00:14:28.643
Garrett: I can't believe you
actually want me to comment

00:14:28.643 --> 00:14:32.510
on the scientific realism of "Spaceballs."

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:34.520
They're flying around space in a Winnebago

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:37.410
with a dogman as your second in command,

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:40.430
and, really, you want me to
talk about whether or not

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:41.620
it violates the laws of physics.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:42.950
Where do I start?

00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:45.280
First issue is if you're traveling

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.810
in between planets, even
between solar systems

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:50.340
in the future, so, once
you're on your trajectory,

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:52.090
you're not burning your engines anymore.

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:53.680
For example, when we went to the moon,

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:56.690
we got into Earth orbit,
we lit the engines up

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:59.810
to get enough velocity
to escape Earth orbit,

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:01.540
and get on a trajectory
going towards the moon.

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:03.120
But that only lasted minutes.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:05.210
And then, once we had that velocity

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:06.540
and we were heading in the right direction

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:08.670
at the right speed, we
shut the engines down,

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:09.850
and you coast.

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:11.690
So, if you run out of gas, you don't fall

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:12.580
out of the sky. [laughs]

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:14.354
You're going to continue coasting

00:15:14.354 --> 00:15:15.860
in the same direction and the same

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:17.620
trajectory you had before.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:20.780
But, again, you know,
it's a Winnebago. [laughs]

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:23.830
So what do you want me to tell you?

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:25.360
Anyway, if you can accept all that

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:30.220
and you run out of fuel, now
you fall, which is wrong.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:32.738
And now you have to
crash-land on this planet,

00:15:32.738 --> 00:15:33.800
and it's a desert.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:35.700
That can happen. You know,
Earth has got deserts.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:36.980
Obviously, Earth is a planet.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.500
Mars and the moon both have
a lot of sand-like soil

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:43.170
we call regolith, so it's not unrealistic

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:44.710
that if you came down on a planet

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:47.510
that there would be a
desert with lots of sand.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:52.690
Spock: Gravity systems
are failing. Hold on.

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:54.490
Scotty: There won't be time for evacuation

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:57.341
if we don't get power to
stabilize the d--- ship!

00:15:57.341 --> 00:15:59.373
Kirk: Scotty, we've got to get
the power back on, come on!

00:15:59.373 --> 00:16:00.950
Garrett: When you're way out in the future

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:03.720
and you're, like, "Star
Trek" time, super-advanced

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.130
with photon torpedoes
and phasers and stuff

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:08.220
and green women, you can just speculate

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:09.500
that you've got some advanced technology.

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:12.170
So, in the "Star Trek" world, we just have

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:14.200
gravity plate that is degrading,

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:16.100
according to Spock in this clip,

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:18.240
and you can just wave
your hands and do that.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:19.280
The thing about that is,

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:20.113
so, let me tell you a story.

00:16:20.113 --> 00:16:21.440
I was up on the space station,

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:23.713
and when you're up on the space station,

00:16:23.713 --> 00:16:25.950
they ask you, "Would you
like to talk to anybody,"

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:28.390
like, any celebrities
or interesting people.

00:16:28.390 --> 00:16:30.840
And they do that as
kind of a morale boost.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:32.698
So I said, "Yeah, I would like to talk to

00:16:32.698 --> 00:16:35.090
Ron Moore and David Eick."

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:38.130
They are the people that
created "Battlestar Galactica."

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:39.630
We had this video teleconference,

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:42.217
and I said to them, "You
know, you did the same thing.

00:16:42.217 --> 00:16:44.757
You come up with this
artificial gravity on Galactica.

00:16:44.757 --> 00:16:46.757
Everybody's just walking
around like they're on Earth,

00:16:46.757 --> 00:16:47.807
but you're out in the middle of space.

00:16:47.807 --> 00:16:49.337
Where's the gravity coming from?

00:16:49.337 --> 00:16:50.527
Why do you have to do that?

00:16:50.527 --> 00:16:53.107
Like, why would you take
away one of the coolest

00:16:53.107 --> 00:16:56.130
things about being up
here, the ability to fly?"

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:57.677
And Ron Moore said to me, he said,

00:16:57.677 --> 00:17:01.077
"Garrett...you have any idea how expensive

00:17:01.077 --> 00:17:03.690
those special effects are
and all those stunts?"

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:05.670
So I get why, especially
if you're doing something

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:07.906
like "Battlestar Galactica" or "Star Trek"

00:17:07.906 --> 00:17:11.840
that you'd want to just
say oh, artificial gravity.

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:13.907
So I'm not gonna knock "Star Trek" down.

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:19.010
News anchor: The deadly
CO2 gas is literally

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:21.900
poisoning the astronauts
with every breath in and out.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:26.217
Control: OK, we have an
unusual procedure for you here.

00:17:26.217 --> 00:17:27.416
Fred Haise: They want you
to rip the cover off the

00:17:27.416 --> 00:17:28.908
flight plan.

00:17:28.908 --> 00:17:30.153
Jack Swigert: With pleasure.

00:17:30.153 --> 00:17:31.436
Control: All right, now, the
other materials you're gonna

00:17:31.436 --> 00:17:34.919
need here are two lithium
hydroxide canisters,

00:17:34.919 --> 00:17:36.336
duct tape.

00:17:36.336 --> 00:17:39.070
You want to cut the duct tape 3 feet long.

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:40.540
Swigert: Houston, filter's in place.

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:42.060
Garrett: So, yes, that really happened.

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:43.643
They had a big challenge because they had

00:17:43.643 --> 00:17:47.720
to find some way of scrubbing
out the carbon dioxide

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:50.850
inside the command module of Apollo 13

00:17:50.850 --> 00:17:52.840
without having it powered up.

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:54.790
So, humans make carbon dioxide.

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:55.810
We consume oxygen.

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:57.590
We breathe out carbon dioxide.

00:17:57.590 --> 00:17:59.480
And if that gets too
high, bad things happen.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:00.422
You get headaches,

00:18:00.422 --> 00:18:02.070
and eventually you can even die

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:04.580
if the carbon dioxide levels get too high.

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:07.060
Now, on Earth we have a wonderful thing,

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:09.810
it's called plants [laughs],
that do this for us.

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:12.560
And during Apollo 13,
or in any spacecraft,

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:15.680
you have to somehow accomplish
it without having plants.

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:19.290
And so we use a chemical
called lithium hydroxide.

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:21.787
It absorbs the CO2, but you use it up.

00:18:21.787 --> 00:18:23.530
Like, on the space
shuttle, we had to change

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:25.430
out the canisters of LiOH,

00:18:25.430 --> 00:18:27.810
lithium hydroxide, every so often.

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:29.470
So they couldn't use the equipment

00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:31.498
inside the command module that normally

00:18:31.498 --> 00:18:33.510
takes all the carbon
dioxide that we spew out

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:36.460
and sucks that and scrubs
that out of the air.

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:37.840
John Young: There's been an explosion.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.840
Oxygen tanks are gone,
two fuel cells gone,

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:41.200
command module's shut down.

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:42.390
News anchor: And astronauts Jim Lovell,

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:44.460
Fred Haise, and Jack Swigert are making

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.695
their way through the
tunnel to the lunar module,

00:18:46.695 --> 00:18:48.575
using it as a lifeboat so they'll have

00:18:48.575 --> 00:18:50.050
electrical power for their radios

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:51.387
on the command module.

00:18:51.387 --> 00:18:52.715
Garrett: They had to
find some way to do it

00:18:52.715 --> 00:18:56.040
using the lunar lander,
which wasn't intended

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:58.280
to be used for that period
of time for three people.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.040
News anchor: With each
breath, the three men expel

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:03.448
more of the poisonous gas
into the lunar module cockpit,

00:19:03.448 --> 00:19:06.200
and the scrubbers intended to
keep the atmosphere breathable

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:08.060
are quickly becoming saturated.

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:09.800
Garrett: They had to use
some of the canisters

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:11.120
from the command module.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:12.737
The thing was, the command module

00:19:12.737 --> 00:19:15.240
and the lunar module were made
by two different companies.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:16.838
Because they were two different companies,

00:19:16.838 --> 00:19:18.600
they actually made them
two different shapes.

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:20.560
So one was round, and one was square.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:22.440
Well, they came up with
a pretty good plan,

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:23.850
which was that they made an adapter

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:26.410
by using the cardboard or the cover

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:28.710
of the flight plan, some duct tape,

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:31.280
and some hose, and they found a way to

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:32.520
make an adapter to use

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:34.878
this square peg in the round hole.

00:19:34.878 --> 00:19:36.480
That's really the workaround

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:38.077
that they came up with, and it worked.

00:19:38.077 --> 00:19:40.960
And so what you see in
that movie is 100% real.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:43.475
They even used actual dialog.

00:19:43.475 --> 00:19:46.110
You know, NASA recorded
all the transmissions

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:48.874
to and from the spaceship
and on the intercom,

00:19:48.874 --> 00:19:50.390
and so they had transcripts of what

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:52.670
actually happened during
the real Apollo 13.

00:19:52.670 --> 00:19:53.750
[alarm buzzing]

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:55.960
Swigert: Hey, we've got a problem here.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:57.560
Control: Uh, this is Houston.

00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:58.630
Say again, please.

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:00.030
Jim Lovell: Houston, we have a problem.

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:02.350
Garrett: "Apollo 13" is the gold standard.

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:04.170
They got it right more than probably any

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:06.300
other movie ever made about space.

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:09.693
This movie is the closest
thing to being a documentary

00:20:09.693 --> 00:20:12.150
without actually taking cameras
up and filming it in space.

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:18.167
Stephen Colbert: I've heard that you,

00:20:18.167 --> 00:20:20.960
in space, no one can hear you scream.

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:22.910
Would you test that for me right now?

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:25.120
Could you scream for us?

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:26.903
Garrett: Sure, Stephen, I'd be happy to.

00:20:28.158 --> 00:20:29.560
[audience laughing]

00:20:29.560 --> 00:20:30.393
Colbert: It's true!

00:20:32.420 --> 00:20:33.253
It's true!

00:20:33.253 --> 00:20:35.141
No one can hear you scream!

